        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "f52c1e73-b9c7-5243-a098-f5220729d383",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jim Eastwood, Roy Knight, Dr. Alan Cooke\n- Places: Cookstown, County Tyrone, Northern Ireland\n- Organizations: University of Ulster, Harvard, University of North Carolina, Archbishop of Armagh\n- Events: The Apprentice, Flight of the Earls, Plantation of Ulster\n\nFacts:\n- Cookstown is a town and townland in County Tyrone, Northern Ireland.\n- After the Flight of the Earls during the Plantation of Ulster, the Archbishop of Armagh was granted the lands in the Cookstown area.\n- Around 1620, Dr. Alan Cooke leased the townlands in the Cookstown area from the Archbishop of Armagh, and Cookstown was founded around that time.\n- Jim Eastwood, also known as Charles James Eastwood, was born in Cookstown, County Tyrone, Northern Ireland.\n- Roy Knight was born in Cookstown, County Tyrone, Northern Ireland.\n- Jim Eastwood graduated from the University of Ulster and also attended Harvard for a two-week course and the University of North Carolina.\n- Roy Knight worked as a farmer and teacher.\n- Jim Eastwood is a Northern Irish businessman.\n- Jim Eastwood was formerly one of the final four contestants in the seventh UK series of The Apprentice.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "dcd55393-4c20-50ba-9c5a-260291a30166",
        "text": "Page: Jim Eastwood. Sentence: Charles James Eastwood, better known as Jim Eastwood, is a Northern Irish Businessman and formerly one of the final four contestants in the seventh UK series of \"The Apprentice\".\nPage: Jim Eastwood. Sentence: He was born in Cookstown, County Tyrone, Northern Ireland and is a graduate of the University of Ulster having also attended Harvard for a two-week course and the University of North Carolina.\nPage: Cookstown. Sentence: Cookstown is a town and townland in County Tyrone, Northern Ireland.\nPage: Cookstown. Sentence: It was founded around 1620 when the townlands in the area were leased by an English ecclesiastical lawyer, Dr. Alan Cooke, from the Archbishop of Armagh, who had been granted the lands after the Flight of the Earls during the Plantation of Ulster.\nPage: Roy Knight. Sentence: He was born in Cookstown, County Tyrone, Northern Ireland and became a farmer and teacher by career.",
